{"step_index":1945,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-27T07:20:16Z","input_tokens":3786,"cache_read_tokens":159099,"output_tokens":3522,"thinking":"Upgrading the topics definition to `TOPIC_METADATA_30` within `dsa_dashboard.js` seems appropriate. Currently, the goal is to define and list all 30 topics along with associated metadata.\n\n\nData structures and techniques are being cataloged, starting with \"Strings,\" \"Hashing and Hash Maps.\" Then, \"Two Pointers,\" \"Sliding Window,\" and \"Prefix Sum\" are also added. The intention is to capture a wide array of topics, covering data structures, algorithmic techniques, and related patterns.\n\n\n\"Binary Search\" is a divide-and-conquer strategy, reducing search space logarithmically, suitable for sorted data. \"Sorting\" allows use of two pointers and facilitates other algorithmic approaches. \"Linked Lists\" are added as linear chains of nodes, and \"Stacks\" as LIFO structures.\n\"Queues and Deques\" are added, focusing on FIFO and double-ended queues for breadth-first search and sliding window operations. \"Recursion\" is added as a self-referential technique.\n\n\n\"Backtracking\" is added to the techniques, highlighting depth-first exploration with pruning. \"Trees and Binary Trees\" are added, focusing on hierarchical structures and traversal methods. \"Binary Search Trees\" are added, emphasizing sorted order and logarithmic search.\n\n\"Heaps and Priority Queues\" are incorporated, focusing on root-based element ordering for efficient retrieval. Finally, \"Graphs\" are included, modeling networks and their applications in various problems.\n\n\nBreadth-First Search (BFS) is added as an algorithm, utilizing a FIFO queue for layer-by-layer exploration, particularly effective for finding shortest unweighted paths. Depth-First Search (DFS) is also included as a recursive or stack-based algorithm, exploring branches deeply before backtracking, useful for connectivity and pathfinding.\n\nGreedy Algorithms are added, focusing on locally optimal choices for global optima, suitable for problems with optimal substructure. Dynamic Programming is added next, for solving overlapping subproblems via memoization or DP tables to avoid redundant computation.\nBit Manipulation is added to cover low-level operations for space-efficient techniques. Finally, Tries are incorporated as a tree-based data structure for efficient prefix-based operations.\n\n\nPrefix Trees are added as a tree-based structure optimized for string storage and search, offering O(L) time complexity, where L is the word length. Union-Find is included as a disjoint-set data structure with amortized near-O(1) union and find operations, effective for cycle detection and component analysis in graphs. Topological Sorting is added as an algorithm for ordering vertices in a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) for dependency resolution.\n\nIntervals are included to represent and process contiguous ranges, using techniques like sorting, overlap detection, merging, and intersection finding. Monotonic Stack and Queue is added as a data structure maintaining order (increasing/decreasing) to solve next greater/smaller element and sliding window maximum in O(n) and O(1) respectively. Finally, Math and Number Theory is included for concepts such as modulo arithmetic and prime number computation.\n\n\nThe \"Math and Number Theory\" topic is expanded to include prime number generation (Sieve of Eratosthenes), Greatest Common Divisor (Euclidean algorithm), efficient exponentiation, and digit manipulation techniques.  Matrix Problems are added with 2D grid traversals, in-place rotations, spiral orders, subgrid calculations, and flood-fill island discoveries. Advanced Data Structures are also incorporated, including LRU/LFU caches, Segment Trees, Binary Indexed Trees (Fenwick trees), and median finders using dual heaps.\n\n\nThe dedicated topic page's structure is being defined, starting with breadcrumbs for navigation and a hero header to introduce the topic. This header includes a summary, metrics for user progress, and a difficulty distribution bar. The \"Recommended Next Problem\" card displays an unsolved problem, alongside actions to solve and bookmark it.\n\nAn interactive toolbar is being designed to allow filtering and browsing of problems within the topic, focusing on difficulty, algorithmic pattern, learning status, and sorting options. The filtered problem table will then display the problems with multi-topic badges, bookmarking functionality, and problem details like title, pattern, and difficulty.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"C:\\Users\\CVT\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\code-flow-visualizer\\app_build\\dsa_dashboard.js","EndLine":250,"StartLine":150,"toolAction":"Reviewing DsaDashboard methods","toolSummary":"View method definitions in dsa_dashboard.js"}}]}
